hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in the course of  his work on fate where Cicero is  discussing whether everything is in fact  determined or whether we have some  measure of freedom of choice or of the  will he discusses something that has  been called by convention the idle  argument it already had a name by the  time that Cicero encounters it both in  Greek and in Latin in Greek it is the  Argos logos and in Cicero's Latin it is  the Ignacio and when we say argument  here you don't necessarily want to think  of something in syllogistic form but  think of a line of argumentation really  having to do with our practical  reasoning about what we ought to do or  what we ought not to do and it's  interesting if you put it that way  because it's really saying is its  conclusion don't do anything because  nothing that you do makes any difference  or if you want the opposite of that do  whatever you want because nothing you do  makes any difference and how do we get  there so Cicero is going to give us a  formulation of this that has to do with  somebody being sick and the question is  should they call for the doctor or  now and notice that both sides of the  argument begin with the same if-then  statement if it is faded and then  something follows after that then and  then something follows after that so  what are the two sides if it's faded for  you to recover from this illness then  you will recover from the illness  whether you bring in a doctor or not and  you could expand this to other things  whether you consult WebMD whether you  lay in bed all day or whether you go out  drinking no matter what you do if you're  fated to recover from that illness you  will recover from that illness on the  other hand the statement runs if it's  fated for you to not recover from that  illness then you won't recover from that  illness and that holds whether you bring  in a doctor or don't bring in a doctor  we could extend this to other things as  well  again maybe you bring in a doctor but  you don't listen to the doctor's advice  we could go on and on and on and you  could say this about other things to  think about whether you're going to find  the person who is the ideal mate for you  if you're going to find that person it  doesn't matter whether you go out on  dates or not you will find that person  if it's faded and if it's faded that you  won't find that person then it doesn't  matter how many dates you go on you'll  never find that person and so what is  the conclusion of this do whatever you  want don't do anything it doesn't matter  what you do because it's already faded  that it's going to go one way or another  so nothing that you as a human agent can  do within the situation will actually  make any contribution to a different  outcome  so given most people's propensity  towards the sort of laziness and  indolence Cicero actually says you can  not only call this the Ignazio but also  the  there's meaning just gonna sit there  doing nothing this is an argument for  doing nothing an argument for as we  called it fatalism and people have this  sort of view about all sorts of things  in their life  Cicero goes on to note another feature  of this you can 